-- title:
--   Strong multiplicity one for p-adic Hecke characters on J₁(M)
-- statement:
--   Fix $M\ge 1$, a Dirichlet character $\varepsilon$ modulo $M$ with values in $\mathbb{C}$, and a weight-$2$ cusp form $g$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$ which is a primitive form of nebentypus $\varepsilon$: its $q$-expansion satisfies $a_1(g)=1$, the relations $a_{\ell n}(g)+\varepsilon(\ell)\ell\,[\ell\mid n]\,a_{n/\ell}(g)=a_\ell(g)a_n(g)$ for primes $\ell\nmid M$, the relations $a_{\ell n}(g)=a_\ell(g)a_n(g)$ for primes $\ell\mid M$, it has nebentypus $\varepsilon$, and its eigenvalue packet $(a_n(g),\varepsilon(n))$ occurs at no level $M'\mid M$ with $M'\ne M$. Fix a prime $p$, the hypotheses [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll M`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) (the Hecke inputs along every prime for $X_1(M)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, together with, for each $d$ coprime to $M$, a diamond automorphism of the function field of $X_1(M)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and a base change of it to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$) and [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar M`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54) (the Hecke and diamond generators commute pairwise), a finite set $S\subseteq\mathbb{N}$, a field $\Omega$ that is a $\mathbb{Q}_p$-algebra, a commutative ring $R$, an injective ring homomorphism $t\colon R\to\mathbb{C}$, a ring homomorphism $\psi\colon R\to\Omega$, and $b,e\colon\mathbb{N}\to R$ with $t(b_\ell)=a_\ell(g)$ and $t(e_\ell)=\varepsilon(\ell)$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$. Let $r$ be a prime and $c,d\in R$ with $d$ integral over $\mathbb{Z}$ and $t(c)=\overline{a_r(g)}\,t(d)$. Equip $J_1(M)=\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the base-changed function field of $X_1(M)$ with the Hecke-algebra module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar M`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129). Then every ring homomorphism $\Lambda$ from the $\mathbb{Q}_p$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{Q}_p}(\mathbb{Q}_p\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p}T_pJ_1(M))$ generated by the Hecke and diamond operators to $\Omega$ which satisfies $\Lambda(T_\ell)\psi(e_\ell)=\psi(b_\ell)$ and $\Lambda(\langle\ell\rangle)\psi(e_\ell)=1$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$ also satisfies $\Lambda(T_r)\psi(d)=\psi(c)$.
--
--   This is strong multiplicity one in the form in which it is used here: a character of the Hecke–diamond algebra acting on $V_p J_1(M)$ that matches the eigenvalue packet of a primitive form $g$ at all but finitely many primes away from the level is forced to take the value $\overline{a_r(g)}$ at the $r$-th Hecke operator for every prime $r$, including those dividing $M$, the comparison being expressed through the auxiliary ring $R$ and the homomorphisms $t$ and $\psi$ so that no embedding of the coefficient field into $\Omega$ need be chosen. It is invoked in the determination of the $U_q$-eigenvalue for primes $q$ exactly dividing the level and not dividing the conductor of the nebentypus.
